[
 {:seon.task/name "a-processs-heap-ceiling-fits-the-target-machine", :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-14 (deps): the standing root's heap ceiling is 32 GB (jolt-heap-ceiling-bytes 34,359,738,368), twice Seon's 16 GB target machine (AGENTS section 8), because root never calls seon.lib/heap-cap! and runs on Jolt's default. Root's live heap read 1.42 GB then, so the ceiling bounds nothing it should. Change: every process Seon starts (root, the fork server, agents, test children) takes its heap ceiling from one config key sized to the target machine, read through the one resolver, set at its one start; reaching it is the typed OutOfMemoryError it already is. Done: each process's jolt-heap-ceiling-bytes is that key's value. docs/machine.md#what-a-process-owns-in-memory.", :seon.task/proofs #{seon.lib-test/every-process-starts-under-the-target-machines-heap-ceiling}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-process-the-binary-starts-runs-as-production"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by deps via tests: a test shard runs Datahike's per-restore log/trace through trove's default *log-fn*, which production turns off, so tests measure a path production does not run. What exists: a process's start is written in four partial copies. The binary: gc-sweepers! 0 and end-with! the parent in seon.main/-main (main.clj:170-172). Root, after -main: ignore-sigpipe!, trove/set-log-fn! nil unless :log?, catch-uncaught!, raise-open-files!, sweepers from config, max-heap-free-ratio! in seon.system/start! (system.clj:1748-1781). A test shard: gc-sweepers! 0 only (test/test_runner.clj:874). One test namespace: a hand patch trove/set-log-fn! nil at load (test/seon/procs_test.clj:15). Forked processes (agents, test children, clones, the fork server's standby) inherit the parent's log fn, SIGPIPE disposition and sweeper pool, each adding only its role's steps (heap-cap!, intr/install!, intr/forked!, pdb/after-fork!), so a fork needs nothing from this change. The fix is one fn, seon.lib/start-process!, called first by every process started from the binary: (1) gc-sweepers! 0; (2) end-with! the parent when it is not launchd; (3) ignore-sigpipe!; (4) Datahike/trove logging set as production sets it (off); (5) the uncaught handler pointed at the process's own sink. Callers: seon.main/-main (every binary mode) and test_runner/-main. Root's start! keeps only open files, sweepers from config, heap-free ratio, and :log? turning logging on for checks that ask. The order is the physics (sweepers before anything collects), stated once in the fn, not a hand list. Deletes: test_runner/-main's sweeper call, system/start!'s sigpipe and log-fn lines, procs_test's log-fn patch, and the dead :noise flood (agent_proc.clj:216-220, :313, :319; system.clj:877). Reach: bin/system start, bin/update, bin/test, bin/prove, bin/check, the standing system, worktree systems, test children by inheritance; shared machinery, so one real bin/prove end to end before it lands. Proof, red first: in a shard, trove's *log-fn* is nil and SIGPIPE is ignored; red today. Estimate: two reads of process state, ~10k ticks; no per-call cost. Lands alone, first (before the Flow standard)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.lib-test/a-process-the-binary-starts-runs-as-production seon.system-test/the-sweeper-pool-is-fixed-before-anything-collects}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-forks-wait-ends-at-its-declared-bound-naming-its-holders"
  :seon.task/plan "Approved by root 2026-11-14 (deps). What exists: seon.lib/fork! calls jolt.host/fork-alone, which waits in Chez's S_fork_alone until every other thread parks; only jolt.host/fork-alone-cancel! from another thread ends the wait, and no production caller sets one (agent clones agent.clj:2041, test children agent.clj:1331, the fork server fork_server.clj:47 and :402). A thread in a plain blocking foreign call never parks, so such a fork waits for good: a test child sat 20 min in S_fork_alone until bin/stalls ended it (runs/evidence/gate-stall-1346; that one's cause, a lost wakeup, is fixed in Chez 3ec3794d, but the unbounded wait remains). Change: (1) Chez S_fork_alone takes a deadline and waits with s_thread_cond_timedwait, answering -3 at it, the wake still each park (no polling); (2) jolt.host/fork-alone takes the bound in ms and, at -3, answers the holders: each active thread's id, Jolt name and whether it is in C, read from Chez's thread list ($thread-list, the tc's active and native-state fields); (3) the bound is :seon.config.process/fork-wait-ms, documented, read through the one resolver; fork!'s refused fn takes {:active-threads n :holders [...] :bound-ms ms}, and the existing typed errors (:seon.test/fork-refused, :seon.fork-server/standby-failed, the clone's :clone-failed) carry :holders. Deletes: fork-alone-cancel! as a bound (it stays only if a caller needs to end a wait early; none does today), the test's spinning canceller. Reach: every fork (agents, test children, clones, the fork server, bin/test shards); shared machinery, so one real bin/prove end to end. Estimate: a fork's wait beside parked threads is microseconds; the bound's value from measured waits (max over a gate run) times 10, not a guess. Proof, red first: a thread in a plain blocking read holds the fork; fork! answers refused within the bound, its holders naming that thread as in C. docs/failure-points.md (the fork row)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.lib-test/a-forks-wait-ends-at-its-bound-naming-its-holders}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}

 {:seon.task/name "a-process-reports-the-most-processes-it-held-at-once"
  :seon.task/plan "For the effects owner's admission (tests' :process kind, docs/plan.md#the-order-of-work-now item 4): a run's class is weighed by the most processes it held at once, read with no ps (seon.prove/held-processes, one ps read of every process's environment, ~70 ms a process each grant pass, goes with it). What exists [M]: processes start through seon.lib/fork! (agents, test children, clones, the fork server), seon.lib/sh (external programs, synchronous), and bin/held (roots a test starts, detached: re-parented to launchd, so a walk of the process tree misses them, and a test's roots and fork servers lead their own groups, bin/held:86/92, fork_server.clj:339, so a pgid count misses them too). A count taken at the run's end is no peak: its processes are gone. Design: each Seon process counts what it starts in the one spawn path, live (seon.lib: +1 at fork!/sh start, -1 at its exit, the exit already an event: jolt.io-poller/exit-chan), keeping its peak as 1 + the most of its children's sum at once, each child counted at the peak it reports. A child reports its own peak in the end message its starter already reads: an agent's :seon.process/end (seon.agent's ends channel, the fork server's reap), a root's ROOT STOPPED line (read by seon.test-store's stop of the roots a test starts), a test child's :done; an external program counts 1. The test child's peak rides :done as :seon.fx/peak-processes. An upper bound (children's peaks summed though they may not coincide), which is safe for admission. Proof, red first: seon.lib-test/a-process-reports-the-most-processes-it-held-at-once, a child that starts two processes at once, then one, reports 3 (itself and two), and a run with no children reports 1. Estimate: one counter update per spawn and exit (~1k ticks), the proof ~2M ticks. Builder runtime; reader tests (admission). Found while building [M, read 2026-11-14]: (1) a test process's shared roots (seon.test-store/shared-root!) outlive the test that started them, so their processes are the shard's, not one test's: a shared root's peak weighs its shard's admission, once; (2) root already holds what it needs: the api proc's state follows every agent process it forks and reaps (pid, forked, ended), so root's peak (itself, its fork server, the most agents live at once) is a fold in that proc's state, no new counter, printed on its ROOT STOPPED line beside :ms. Order of slices: (a) root's peak on ROOT STOPPED, read by seon.test-store/stop-root!; (b) seon.lib/sh and fork! count a process's own children, and the test child's peak is 1 + its sh children at once + the peaks of the roots it stopped, riding :done; (c) a shared root's peak weighs its shard, from its stop at the shard's end."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.lib-test/a-process-reports-the-most-processes-it-held-at-once}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}
]
